@page "/learn/quickgrid-url-paging"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Grid state in the URL · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Grid state in the URL</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You sort a grid and go to page 2, then refresh. Why are you back on page 1?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Static SSR · about 4 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> QuickGrid keeps its sort and page in the URL. Its headers and pager are links,
        so they work on a static page, and you can share any view as a link. In .NET 10, on a static page, the
        headers and pager did nothing.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a products grid on a static page, with sortable columns and a pager. We asked it for
        <code>?sort=Price&amp;direction=desc&amp;page=2</code>: page 2, highest price first.
    </p>
    <p>
        The old grid can't run in this app, so Fig. 1 replays what .NET 10 sent back. It uses its own sample
        products, so its rows won't match Fig. 2. Compare what each grid does with the URL, not the rows.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A sortable grid on a static page, recorded on .NET 10" Url="demo/grid-url/products/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            The URL at the top of the frame is what we asked .NET 10 for. The grid ignored it: page 1, sorted by Product.
            Click <b>Price</b> or <b>›</b> and nothing happens at all.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The buttons do nothing</h2>
    <p>Here's what .NET 10 sent for the Price header and the next-page arrow:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The markup .NET 10 returned</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;button class="col-title" type="button"&gt;Price&lt;/button&gt;   <span class="err">no handler</span>
&lt;button class="go-next" type="button"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;          <span class="err">no handler</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        These are buttons that expect C# code to run when you click them. On a static page, there's no live
        connection to the server to run that code, so the click goes nowhere. The grid also keeps its sort and page
        to itself and never reads the URL. So a refresh, or a shared link, always starts on page 1.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Upgrade. That's it.</h2>
    <p>There's nothing to turn on. A normal QuickGrid and Paginator on a static page is the whole fix:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix: the grid you'd write anyway</span>
        <pre class="snip">@@page "/demo/grid-url/products"
<span class="dim">@@* no @@rendermode, so the page is static SSR *@@</span>

&lt;QuickGrid Items="products" Pagination="pagination"&gt;
    &lt;PropertyColumn <span class="hl">Title="Price"</span> Property="p =&gt; p.UnitPrice"
                    <span class="hl">Sortable="true"</span> /&gt;
&lt;/QuickGrid&gt;
&lt;Paginator State="pagination" /&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, QuickGrid reads <code>sort</code>, <code>direction</code> and <code>page</code> from the URL.
        Each sortable header and pager arrow is a link to the next view. A click just loads a new URL.
        <code>sort=Price</code> picks the column whose <code>Title</code> is Price. Here's the same URL on .NET 11.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same query string, on .NET 11" Url="demo/grid-url/products?sort=Price&direction=desc&page=2"
                Label=".NET 11 · live" Side="after">
        <Caption>
            This time the grid follows the URL: page 2, highest price first. Click <b>Price</b> or <b>‹</b> and watch
            the address change. Press <b>⟳</b> and you stay on the same view.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the page behind Fig. 2:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/GridUrl/Products.razor" Feature="quickgrid-url-paging">
        <Caption>
            No render mode, and no sorting or paging code. Fig. 1 is a recording of this same kind of grid on .NET 10.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Go interactive, or write it yourself</h2>
    <p>Here's the same grid in .NET 10, built two ways:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The same grid in .NET 10, and what it did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@page "/static"</pre>
                <span class="why">headers and pager are buttons with no handler; sort and page in the URL are ignored</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ clicks do nothing</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@page "/interactive"
<span class="err">@@rendermode InteractiveServer</span></pre>
                <span class="why">clicks work over a live connection to the server, but the URL never changes</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a refresh or shared link lands on page 1, sorted by Product</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Going interactive keeps a live connection, and server memory, open for every visitor, just to sort a table.
        The other option was to drop QuickGrid's sorting and pager. You'd read <code>sort</code> and
        <code>page</code> with <code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</code>, then write your own header links and
        <code>OrderBy</code>, <code>Skip</code> and <code>Take</code> for every grid.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The URL holds the view</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 every QuickGrid works this way. On a static page, headers and pager are links, so a click loads
        the new URL. On an interactive page, the grid updates the URL as you click.
    </p>
    <p>
        Because it's always on, your existing grids change when you upgrade. Sortable headers become
        <code>&lt;a class="col-title"&gt;</code> links instead of buttons, so CSS aimed at
        <code>button.col-title</code> stops working. You can switch back to buttons; see below.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Sorting matches the column title.</b> Rename a column, and old links that sort by it fall back to the default sort.</li>
        <li><b>Two grids on one page?</b> Give each a prefix: <code>new QueryParameterNameOptions("cities_")</code> makes them <code>cities_sort</code>, <code>cities_direction</code> and <code>cities_page</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Turning it off.</b> To get the buttons back, add <code>AppContext.SetSwitch("Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.QuickGrid.EnableUrlBasedQuickGridNavigationAndSorting", false);</code> at the top of <code>Program.cs</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Your own query parameters stay.</b> A filter read with <code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</code> is kept in the grid's links.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
